    "done_when": "A report at ~/.config/reckon/crew/reports/reckon/s21-host-placement-block.md, under 40 KB, gives the exact YAML placement block to add to every CLI backend in the host flight layer, /home/ITER/mcintos/Code/dotfiles/reckon/flight.yaml. The backends are codex, codex-spark, codex-luna, codex-terra, codex-astra, clive, claude, claude-opus and claude-haiku; native is excluded and the report says why. Each block validates against reckon/schema/flight.yaml's PlacementConfig: scheduler, options, job_id_probe, state_query, reason_query, and requirements naming the paths and endpoints a worker needs, including the clive router endpoint. The report shows the block resolving. It copies flight.yaml to a scratch path, adds the blocks, and points reckon at the copy through whichever environment variable reckon/flight.py reads for the host layer, named at file and line. `reckon flight` then shows each backend's resolved placement, and `reckon crew dispatch --dry-run` for one clive node and one codex node shows the composed launch argv. That argv names srun with --jobid=1277272 and --overlap, the job the unkeyed reservation record at ~/.config/reckon/crew/placement/reservation.json names. The report states which srun the launch resolves on this node, /usr/bin/srun or reckon/host_shims/srun, at file and line, and whether the shim changes the composed step. It states what a dispatch from a login node outside the allocation would compose. And it lists every way the declaration could refuse or break a dispatch: an unmet requirement, a missing state_query, the roster cap of 25 against the live pointer count, or the fence. No real scheduler verb runs except read-only squeue and scontrol queries by job id. No dispatch is made for real. Neither flight.yaml nor any repository file changes. Every command and its output is logged under the run directory, ending EXIT=.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
